<p>Recently I was developing a live-streaming app, and I found that once CPU usage maxed out, the CPU downclocked, overheating got severe, and then came the stutter…</p>

<p>To pinpoint this problem, we spent at least 3 days tracing the thread code that was consuming CPU, step by step. When we hit C++ threads, there was no symbol table — all we could see was a blob of object addresses, not even a method name. We were completely at a loss. This post introduces an advanced debugging method that uses the symbol table and related instructions to trace the code calls. It’s not the best-written piece, so I hope the masters will go easy on me. Special thanks to my colleague Chen Hao for his strong support with the code process.</p>

<h1 id="talk-is-cheap-show-me-the-code">Talk is cheap show me the code</h1>

<p>Our approach is to find the base address of the dynamic library and start from there, using the relevant instructions to recover the call chain.</p>

